Make the modules JSON cache live in the user's home directory.

`lean.models` refreshes its catalogue of brokerages, data providers and plugins
from the QuantConnect CDN once a day and stores it next to the package, which is
read-only in the Nix store: the packaged CLI could never pick up new modules and
every invocation would make a download attempt that was doomed to fail.

Keep the refreshable copy in `~/.lean` (where the CLI already stores its config,
credentials and cache) and fall back to the snapshot shipped with the package
when there is no cache and no network.
--- a/lean/models/__init__.py
+++ b/lean/models/__init__.py
@@ -19,7 +19,11 @@
 json_modules = {}
 file_name = "modules-1.14.json"
 directory = Path(__file__).parent
-file_path = directory.parent / file_name
+# The Nix store is read-only, so the refreshable copy lives in the user's
+# ~/.lean directory alongside the CLI's other caches, and the copy shipped with
+# the package is the fallback when there is no cache and no network.
+bundled_file_path = directory.parent / file_name
+file_path = Path("~/.lean").expanduser() / file_name
 
 # check if new file is available online
 url = f"https://cdn.quantconnect.com/cli/{file_name}"
@@ -48,7 +52,10 @@
     error = str(e)
     pass
 
-# check if file exists
+# check if file exists, falling back to the copy bundled with the package
+if not Path(file_path).is_file():
+    file_path = bundled_file_path
+
 if not Path(file_path).is_file():
